.SH NAME
ktm-guide-switch \- switch (chapter 6 of the ktm language guide)
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
\fBswitch\fR picks one of several branches by a value. It looks like C's, with four differences: cases don't fall through into each other, a case can be a range, you can switch on text, and an explicit \fBfallthrough\fR is there for the rare case you want it.
.SH The basics
.PP
The value is compared with each \fBcase\fR; the matching case runs. \fBdefault\fR runs when none matches. A case ends where the next one starts, so no \fBbreak\fR is needed.
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
void describe(i64 n) {
    switch (n) {
        case 0: printf("%d: zero\en", n);
        case 1: printf("%d: one\en", n);
        default: printf("%d: many\en", n);
    }
}

void main() {
    describe(0);
    describe(1);
    describe(7);
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
0: zero
1: one
7: many
.EE
.RE
.PP
In C, \fBdescribe(0)\fR would print all three lines. In ktm each case stands alone.
.PP
Without a \fBdefault\fR, a value that matches no case does nothing:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
void main() {
    for (i64 i = 0; i < 4; i++) {
        switch (i) {
            case 1: printf("one ");
            case 3: printf("three ");
        }
    }
    printf("\en");
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
one three 
.EE
.RE
.PP
The parentheses are optional, as for \fBif\fR:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
void main() {
    i64 n = 2;
    switch n {
        case 2: printf("two\en");
        default: printf("other\en");
    }
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
two
.EE
.RE
.SH break
.PP
\fBbreak\fR leaves the switch early, from the middle of a case:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
void check(i64 n) {
    switch (n) {
        default:
            if (n < 0) {
                printf("%d: negative, stop\en", n);
                break;
            }
            printf("%d: fine\en", n);
    }
}

void main() {
    check(\-3);
    check(4);
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
\-3: negative, stop
4: fine
.EE
.RE
.PP
Inside a loop, \fBbreak\fR in a switch leaves the switch, not the loop; \fBcontinue\fR goes to the loop's next pass.
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
void main() {
    for (i64 i = 0; i < 5; i++) {
        switch (i) {
            case 1: continue;
            case 3: break;
            default: printf("%d ", i);
        }
        printf("| ");
    }
    printf("\en");
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
0 | 2 | | 4 | 
.EE
.RE
.SH Several values, one body
.PP
Labels stacked with nothing between them share the code after them.
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
u8 *dayType(i64 day) {
    switch (day) {
        case 0:
        case 6:
            return "weekend";
        default:
            return "weekday";
    }
}

void main() {
    printf("%s %s %s\en", dayType(0), dayType(3), dayType(6));
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
weekend weekday weekend
.EE
.RE
.SH Ranges
.PP
\fBcase lo ... hi:\fR matches every value from \fBlo\fR to \fBhi\fR, both included.
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
void grade(i64 score) {
    switch (score) {
        case 90 ... 100: printf("%d: A\en", score);
        case 80 ... 89:  printf("%d: B\en", score);
        case 0:          printf("%d: no score\en", score);
        default:         printf("%d: C or below\en", score);
    }
}

void main() {
    grade(95);
    grade(83);
    grade(0);
    grade(42);
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
95: A
83: B
0: no score
42: C or below
.EE
.RE
.PP
Ranges work on characters too, since a character is a number:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
u8 *kind(u8 c) {
    switch (c) {
        case 'a' ... 'z': return "lower";
        case 'A' ... 'Z': return "upper";
        case '0' ... '9': return "digit";
        default:          return "other";
    }
}

void main() {
    printf("%s %s %s %s\en", kind('q'), kind('Q'), kind('7'), kind('!'));
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
lower upper digit other
.EE
.RE
.PP
Two cases may not cover the same value:
.PP
This does not compile:
.RS 4
.EX
void main() {
    i64 n = 5;
    switch (n) {
        case 1 ... 10: printf("low\en");
        case 5: printf("five\en");
    }
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
error:
.EE
.RE
.SH fallthrough
.PP
\fBfallthrough;\fR as the last statement of a case runs on into the next case.
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
void main() {
    for (i64 i = 1; i <= 3; i++) {
        printf("%d:", i);
        switch (i) {
            case 1:
                printf(" one");
                fallthrough;
            case 2:
                printf(" one\-or\-two");
            case 3:
                printf(" three");
        }
        printf("\en");
    }
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
1: one one\-or\-two
2: one\-or\-two
3: three
.EE
.RE
.PP
\fBfallthrough\fR is an error anywhere else:
.IP \(bu 2
outside a switch, or before other statements of its case;
.IP \(bu 2
inside an \fBif\fR or a loop in the case;
.IP \(bu 2
in the last case, which has no next case.
.PP
This does not compile:
.RS 4
.EX
void main() {
    i64 n = 1;
    switch (n) {
        case 1:
            fallthrough;
            printf("never\en");
        case 2:
            printf("two\en");
    }
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
error:
.EE
.RE
.SH Each case is a block
.PP
A case can declare its own variables without braces, and two cases can use the same name.
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
void main() {
    for (i64 i = 0; i < 2; i++) {
        switch (i) {
            case 0:
                i64 n = 10;
                printf("case 0: n = %d\en", n);
            case 1:
                i64 n = 20;
                printf("case 1: n = %d\en", n);
        }
    }
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
case 0: n = 10
case 1: n = 20
.EE
.RE
.PP
A \fBdefer\fR in a case runs at the end of that case. It needs braces: \fBcase 1: { defer ...; }\fR\&.
.SH Switching on enums
.PP
The cases of an enum (see ktm-guide-enums(7)) are its members.
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
enum Color { red, green, blue };

u8 *hex(Color c) {
    switch (c) {
        case Color.red:   return "#f00";
        case Color.green: return "#0f0";
        case Color.blue:  return "#00f";
    }
    return "?";
}

void main() {
    printf("%s %s %s\en", hex(Color.red), hex(Color.green), hex(Color.blue));
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
#f00 #0f0 #00f
.EE
.RE
.SH Switching on text
.PP
A \fBu8[]\fR or a \fBString\fR can be switched on, with string literals as the cases. A case matches when the value has exactly its bytes: the same length, the same case, no trimming.
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
i64 run(u8[] cmd) {
    switch (cmd) {
        case "GET":    return 1;
        case "SET":    return 2;
        case "DEL":
        case "REMOVE": return 3;
        default:       return 0;
    }
}

void main() {
    u8[] line = "SET name ktm";
    printf("%d %d %d %d\en", run("GET"), run(line[0:3]), run("REMOVE"), run("get"));
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
1 2 3 0
.EE
.RE
.IP \(bu 2
Every case is a string literal: no ranges and no integers.
.IP \(bu 2
The same string twice is an error.
.IP \(bu 2
The value is computed once, so \fBswitch (readCommand())\fR calls the function once.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBdefault\fR, stacked labels and \fBfallthrough\fR work as in a switch on numbers.
.PP
For a match that ignores case, switch on a lower-case copy:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
import str;

i64 code(u8[] word) {
    String low = str.from(word);
    low.toLower();
    i64 result = 0;
    switch (low) {
        case "yes": result = 1;
        case "no":  result = 2;
    }
    low.free();
    return result;
}

void main() {
    printf("%d %d %d\en", code("YES"), code("No"), code("maybe"));
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
1 2 0
.EE
.RE
.SH Two extras
.SS Implicit case values
.PP
A \fBcase:\fR with no value is the previous case's value plus one, starting from 0.
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
void main() {
    for (i64 i = 0; i < 4; i++) {
        switch (i) {
            case: printf("zero ");
            case: printf("one ");
            case: printf("two ");
            default: printf("more");
        }
    }
    printf("\en");
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
zero one two more
.EE
.RE
.SS switch [x]: no range check
.PP
\fBswitch [x]\fR leaves out the check that \fBx\fR is within the cases' range, for a jump table without the test. Use it only when \fBx\fR always matches a case: anything else is undefined. A switch on text always checks.
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
void main() {
    for (i64 i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
        switch [i] {
            case 0: printf("a");
            case 1: printf("b");
            case 2: printf("c\en");
        }
    }
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
abc
.EE
.RE
